factors of both 100 and 150?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"dda1370929276e10a8bfdeacf75b0f26fda75151786abac77c005ee5fdd9f5f3"},"seconds":6.468137741088867,"seed":23294575141909812,"stop":"eos","text":"Next, we identify the positive two-digit factors of 50. The factors of 50 are:\n\\[\n1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50\n\\]\n\nAmong these, the two-digit factors are:\n\\[\n10, 25, 50\n\\]\n\nThus, there are 3 positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150.\n\nThe final answer is \\boxed{3}."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To determine how many positive two-digit integers are factors of both 100 and 150, we start by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 100 and 150.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 100 and 150:\n\\[\n100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\\[\n150 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor common to both numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(100, 150) = 2^1 \\times 5^2 = 50\n\\]\n\nNext, we identify the positive two-digit factors of 50. The factors of 50 are:\n\\[\n1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50\n\\]\n\nAmong these, the two-digit factors are:\n\\[\n10, 25, 50\n\\]\n\nThus, there are 3 positive two-digit integers that are factors of both 100 and 150.\n\nThe final answer is \\boxed{3}.","id":"prealgebra:567"}
